  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The aurora-crystal is the size of a closed fist. Centuries of sky-light frozen inside it, faint radiance pulsing in a rhythm that has nothing to do with any heart. Seciae~ holds it over the pressure-ice and feeds it fire.
    
    The fire-core's heat enters from below. The crystal's stored light meets it and — the sound. Not shattering. Not singing. Something between; a frequency felt in the sternum before the ears can name it. The ice cracks in a spiral, fracture-lines racing outward in patterns too deliberate for violence, too violent for beauty. Light splits into heat. The air tastes of ozone and mineral char and something older, something the ice was keeping. What emerges from the fracture glows without fuel. Burns without tending. The wrongness lingers in the walls afterward like a voice almost recognized.
    
    It works.
    
    ---
    
    The tunnels of ☽'scar Ramtatus~ branched deeper every week. Seciae~ walked them in the hours after, when the fracture-light still hummed behind their eyes. Their left flipper throbbed where the backlash had caught them — pale lines already forming in the skin, the kind of scar that comes from holding two forces apart with nothing but flesh between.
    
    The colony was new. Everything here was new and smelled of ambition: fresh-carved ice still sweating from the chisel, fire-braziers mounted in alcoves that hadn't existed at last count, the sharp tang of forge-smoke drifting up from galleries where ☽'shadow Covens' Order's engineers argued over charts that were obsolete before the ink dried. Umbra-Princeps Alnae~ Oss had founded this place from settlers off Nightfall Shelf, and the settlers had brought with them the particular hunger of penguins who had lived too long in someone else's tunnels. They carved like they were afraid the ice would freeze back if they stopped.
    
    Seciae~ passed a work crew driving a new passage south. The foreman — Fluxium~, soot-dark and loud — was singing something about fire-cores and freedom, the melody borrowed from an old Nightshelf work-chant but the words rewritten. *We keep what we kindle. We owe what we burn.* Two of the miners wore guild-marks on their wraps: a brazier crossed with a sealed door, the symbol Seciae~ had seen spreading through the forges and mess-halls and shadow-parliaments like heat through stone. They handled their fire-cores bare-flippered, casual, almost tender. They did not touch the aurora-crystals stacked in the supply crate beside them.
    
    Nobody called it doctrine. It was simpler than that. It was the warmth in your flippers when the deep veins ran hot and the fire-cores came up glowing and the surface — the Aurora Stack with its crystal spires and its theology of sky-light — felt as distant as weather. The fire-core veins seemed endless. The deep ice opened like it wanted to be found.
    
    The wrongness was already there, in the walls near Scar☽ Lucamlinaecius. Seciae~ felt it the way you feel a bruise before you see it: a discoloration in the ice that didn't match the colony's charts, a cold that had weight and texture, a whisper-echo from nothing that resolved, if you listened too long, into something that sounded almost like your name. Veil-render, Mairae~ had been down here last season with sanctification rites, burning the corruption back from the Marker's foundations. It had worked. The corruption had retreated to the deeper shafts, the sealed passages, the places that weren't on any chart because Alnae~ Oss had ordered them unmapped.
    
    It had worked the way locking a door works when the thing inside is patient.
    
    Seciae~ pressed their scarred flipper against the tunnel wall. The ice was warm. Not brazier-warm. Not fire-core-warm. A warmth that remembered; a warmth that cost.
    
    ---
    
    Lucatransiius found them sitting in the alcove near the old bore-shaft, the one that dropped straight down into the dark where the volcanic veins pulsed closest. Seciae~ heard the approach before they saw it — the careful footsteps of someone who had learned to announce themselves in a colony where too many penguins startled at shadows.
    
    "Brought you something."
    
    Fire-tea. The cup was chipped volcanic glass, the liquid inside glowing faintly amber from the fire-core shard dissolved in it. Lucatransiius set it down on the ledge between them and said nothing else for a while. That was the way of it between them. Two presences sharing space, briefly.
    
    "Fluxium~'s crew hit a new vein," Lucatransiius said eventually. "South gallery. Fissure-Dealer ☽'Sanctteinvaus says it's the richest yet."
    
    "Everything's the richest yet."
    
    "You sound like the Auditor."
    
    "The Auditor sounds like the tunnels." Seciae~ picked up the tea. Drank. The mineral taste layered over the char of the fire-shard; warmth spreading through them like a proverb half-remembered. "The tunnels say what we want to hear."
    
    Lucatransiius looked at Seciae~'s flipper. The new scars. Said nothing about them. Instead: "Alnae~ Oss wants another demonstration. For the guild-masters. She says if the technique holds—"
    
    "It holds."
    
    "—then the Accord becomes a courtesy. Not a necessity."
    
    Silence. The bore-shaft exhaled warm air from below, carrying with it the faint hum of something geological or something else. The ☽'micseleia' Dagger hung at Seciae~'s hip in its sheath of ancestor-bone, and Lucatransiius's gaze touched it and moved away. The blade was new to Seciae~ then, newly entrusted, the grip not yet worn smooth. It would take years of use for that.
    
    "The ice near the Marker," Seciae~ said. "It's warm again."
    
    "Mairae~ cleansed it."
    
    "I know what Mairae~ did." A pause that carried more than the words around it. "I'm telling you what the ice is doing."
    
    Lucatransiius picked up the fire-tea, took a sip, set it back down between them. The old gesture: what's mine is yours, what's yours is mine, and neither of us will say so.
    
    "The deep ice was left unmapped for reasons," Lucatransiius said quietly.
    
    "The deep ice was left unmapped because no one had the fire to go looking."
    
    "And now we do."
    
    "And now we do."
    
    They sat with that. Two penguins in a colony that was racing to prove it deserved to exist, in tunnels that branched in directions no chart could follow, above a darkness that was not empty but textured — thick, furred, breathing. Somewhere below, the fire-core veins pulsed. Somewhere above, the aurora wasted its light on the sky.
    
    "Still here," Lucatransiius said. An old phrase. A carved phrase.
    
    "Still burning," Seciae~ answered.
    
    The fire-tea went cold between them. They drank it anyway.
    
    ---
    
    The aurora-crystal is the size of a closed fist. Centuries of sky-light frozen inside it, faint radiance pulsing in a rhythm that has nothing to do with any heart. Seciae~ holds it over the pressure-ice and feeds it fire.
    
    The fire-core's heat enters from below. The crystal's stored light meets it and — the sound. Not shattering. Not singing. Something between; a frequency felt in the sternum before the ears can name it. The ice cracks in a spiral, fracture-lines racing outward in patterns too deliberate for violence, too violent for beauty. Light splits into heat. What emerges from the fracture glows without fuel. Burns without tending. The wrongness lingers in the walls afterward like a voice almost recognized.
    
    It works. That's the problem.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771583992278_0", anchor: "then the Accord becomes a courtesy. Not a necessity.", text: "Alnae~ Oss knew better than this rhetoric suggests. The Flipper Accord was already being secretly renegotiated during this period — I have three sequential drafts frozen in administrative ice, each one softening concessions the public version retained. Alnae~ Oss's name appears on the second draft. She was not dreaming of severing the Accord; she was leveraging Fissure☽ Aurora as a bargaining chip to rewrite it. The Way∴al's true believers heard liberation. She heard trade advantage. The chronicler collapses these into one voice. They were not one voice.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771583992278_1", anchor: "a whisper-echo from nothing that resolved, if you listened too long, into something that sounded almost like your name", text: "The chronicler describes textbook veil-thinning and does not seem to know it. During each Long Dark, the boundary between ice-memory and the living world dissolves — the dead speak, visions arrive unbidden, shadow-walkers lose themselves. But this is the Great Thaw, not a Long Dark. If corruption at Scar☽ Lucamlinaecius was thinning the veil independent of seasonal cycles, that is not an atmospheric detail. That is a catastrophic breach indicator. The ice calling your name is not poetry. It is a diagnostic.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771583992278_2", anchor: "They carved like they were afraid the ice would freeze back if they stopped.", text: "The compulsive quality here unsettles me more than the chronicler intends. The Walking seizes penguins who have lost too much — not a decision but a compulsion, a momentum that mimics purpose. These settlers, severed from Nightfall Shelf, carved with that same desperate kinesis. From the outside it looks like ambition. From the ice it looks like something else.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771583992278_3", anchor: "The fire-core veins seemed endless.", text: "They were not. The Rofundis∴rupt would collapse this colony's primary vein. 'Seemed endless' is doing work the chronicler should have made it do harder.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771583992278_4", anchor: "the grip not yet worn smooth. It would take years of use for that.", text: "Years of use. A pristine way to describe what the ☽'micseleia' Dagger did to its bearers. The blade is cursed; the grip wore smooth because Seciae~ could not put it down. 'Use' is not the word. The dagger is currently listed among damaged artifacts awaiting restoration or destruction, and if anyone asks my professional opinion — destruction.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
